    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d150">

                <pb facs="0225" n="211" sameAs="frus:frus1945v09/pg211"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.11/12–1745: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Dominican Republic</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Scherer</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ciudad Trujillo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-12-17T16:00:00-05:00">December 17,
                            1945—4 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:26 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>486. I have just received from the Foreign Minister<anchor n="57"
                        xml:id="d150-f1"/> a copy dated December 14 of his reply to Uruguayan
                    Foreign Minister expressing disapproval of Uruguayan proposal. He states that
                    the proposal of intervention is incompatible with that of national sovereignty
                    and is contrary to positive American international law and would destroy the
                    continental solidarity so brilliantly demonstrated in San Francisco.<anchor
                        n="58" xml:id="d150-f2"/> He says it would be better to use the present
                    Inter-American sanctions and to strengthen them until the spread of disturbing
                    political doctrines is made impossible. He feels that the vitality of the
                    Pan-American system will be sufficient to deal with imperialistic expansion. He
                    concludes that his Government is willing to consult regarding means for
                    protecting human liberty which are contrary to judicial traditions. Text
                    follows. No indication given of date for publication of document.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Scherer</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d150-f1" n="57" xml:id="d150-f1-content">
                <p>Manuel A. Peña Batlle.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d150-f2" n="58" xml:id="d150-f2-content">
                <p>The United Nations Conference on International Organization; for documentation on
                    the Conference, see <ref target="frus1945v01#pg_1">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 1</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
